W4A8 on AIE2: int8 x int4 is a native vmac on this silicon, and int4 storage costs nothing
==========================================================================================

Desktop 2 (DESKTOP-CBL5NUA; Ryzen 7 8700G, Phoenix XDNA1, Windows 11), 2026-09-10, mlir-aie
v1.4.2 ironenv (Peano llvm-aie 22). One core tile: IRON Tile(0, 2), which the trace packet
header reports as row 2, physical column 1. Power mode Default throughout.

QUESTION
--------
TODO 3.2's W4A8 item set a "step zero": build an int4 unpack primitive and confirm it is
single-cycle before making any tile-geometry claim, because docs/SILICON.md (1.2 and 5) lists
int8 x int4 MACs as AIE2p (Strix) only. That listing is SPEC from OGOAT's device.yaml, whose
AIE2 macs_per_cycle table has no int8 x int4 row -- a cost model, the same source whose missing
int16 x int16 row was once read as an ISA statement. aie_api's AIE2 headers define
aie::mmul<M,K,N,int8,int4> (include/aie_api/detail/aie2/mmul_8_4.hpp: shapes 4x16x8, 8x16x8,
4x32x8), and Peano's aiev2/aiev2_vmult.h lowers the 4x16x8 mac to
__builtin_aiev2_I512_I512_ACC1024_acc32_mac_conf -- the same builtin int8 x int8's 4x8x8 mac
uses -- with the B-mode field of the MAC configuration word set to 0 where int8 x int8 sets 1.
So the question became two: does the silicon run that B mode correctly and at one vmac per
cycle, and what does the alternative the item proposed (store int4, unpack to int8 in the core,
run the stock int8 mmul) actually cost.

ANSWER
------
1. int8 x int4 IS native on AIE2. mm_i8i4_native -- aie::mmul<4,16,8,int8,int4>, 512 MACs per
   vmac against int8 x int8's 256 -- matched an int64 reference exactly in every build it was
   run in: 9 builds, 18 processes, each checked on its first call and again on its last timed
   call, 0 mismatches of 4,096 outputs each time. The layout that matches is B packed two per
   byte, element 2i in the LOW nibble, two's complement (MEASURED; the verifier would also have
   reported a match under nibble-swapped or unsigned readings, and none was needed).
   SILICON.md's "int8 x int4 -- AIE2p only" is SUPERSEDED.

2. It issues at one vmac per cycle. native:default's k loop is 16 bundles whose last 8 are
   back-to-back int8 x int4 vmacs (no loads between them); one execution of it measures 17.03
   cycles (the K = 128 -> 256 slope, one execution per unit K). A B mode that held the
   multiplier for two cycles would need at least 24. The latency has a second, independent
   check: this core is statically scheduled and the compiler covers operand latency with
   explicit nops rather than a hardware interlock (tools/aie_disasm.py), so an int4-mode vmac
   slower to produce its result than Peano's machine model assumes would have been read early
   and come out WRONG, not slow. Every output was exact.

3. The marginal k-loop rate on one core goes from 204.8 MAC/cycle (upstream's int8 loop as
   IRON builds it) or 227.6 (the best int8 schedule found here) to 372.4 with the native
   kernel: 1.82x / 1.64x. Per call at 64x256x64 that is 6,295 -> 4,199 cycles (1.50x against
   the control, 1.40x against the best int8); at 64x64x64, where a call is mostly C going in and
   out, 2,447 -> 2,160 (1.13x). Reaching it takes one clang pragma: the native loop is only
   software-pipelined with its k loop unrolled twice (INNER_UNROLL2, a raw pragma in the .cc,
   which survives an IRON build where aie_kernel_utils.h's AIE_LOOP_* hints do not). As IRON
   builds it by default it runs 17.03 cycles per unit K -- 240.5 MAC/cycle, 1.17x -- because
   its 512-bit A operands double the registers a 4x2 expansion holds and the loads never
   overlap the vmacs.

4. The unpack alternative costs nothing and buys only bytes. AIE2's second load unit
   sign-extends int4 to int8 inside the load -- vldb.unpack.s8.s4 -- so a standalone unpack
   loop runs at a plain copy's 2 cycles per 64 elements, and inside the int8 mmul loop (the
   no-unroll build) it is the same 9 bundles and 8 vmacs as int8 and ran exactly that on
   silicon: 18.01 cycles per unit K against 18.0 static. It halves B's bytes in L1 and on the
   wire and adds no MACs. Two compiler outcomes worth knowing: under IRON's default build at
   K=64 the unpack kernel unrolls its k loop fully and spills (113-bundle loop, 1,312 B frame,
   48 stack references inside the loop) -- and still measures 2,119 cycles per call, faster
   than every int8 arm at K=64 and 13 cycles behind native:default's 2,106, because that large
   loop absorbs the C loads (311 cycles outside it against 1,082-1,693 for every other arm); at
   K >= 128 the same default build
   compiles to a clean 32-bundle loop of 32 vmacs and measures 17.01 cycles per unit K, faster
   than any int8 schedule here -- a better unroll, not int4. Read the static "spill" column as a
   schedule shape, not as a verdict.

5. A second, unrelated mechanism runs through every k loop here: extra cycles per loop
   execution that no bundle accounts for, matching the loop's paired loads from ONE buffer.
   The placement settles which pair it cannot be. A, B and C land in three different banks in
   every build (tools/aie_bank_check.py: bank 0 empty, C in bank 1, A and B in banks 2 and 3),
   so a pair reading A and B is cross-bank and costs nothing -- yet the int8 control's single
   paired load costs exactly 1 cycle per iteration. That pair is therefore not A with B; the
   pointer roles name it (both loads advance by one A tile: two rows of A, one bank). The
   positive control is unpack:no-unroll: the same 9 bundles and 8 vmacs, its one paired load
   an A with a B by the same reading, and it measures 0.03 extra cycles per unit K -- zero.
   Where every paired load's pointer role resolves (6 builds) the same-buffer pair count equals
   the measured extra cycles per execution exactly: 1/1, 2/2, 2/2, 2/2, 0/0, 1/1. The 3 native
   builds have unresolved pointers and each sits inside its bounds. None of this contradicts
   the bank validation in docs/BENCHMARKS.md, where separating A from B removed 1,024 cycles
   per panel: that was a different kernel whose paired load read A and B. Upstream mm.cc's own int8 loop, rebuilt with IRON's exact command (at
   64/64/64 that is bundle for bundle the matmul_i8_i32_86901378.o behind the 4607.05 GOPS
   build, section B), has exactly one paired load in its 9-bundle hardware loop, and it is
   `vlda [p4], #0x20 | vldb [p3], #0x20`: both pointers advance by one A tile (0x20), where
   every B pointer in this kernel advances by 0x200 (section D). It reads two rows of A. The
   local control has the same one A&A pair and measured 20.0 cycles per unit K against 18.0
   static, so upstream's loop is predicted -- not measured here -- to take 10 cycles per
   9-bundle iteration, 11% over its schedule. This bears on TODO 3.1's H12: docs/BENCHMARKS.md
   reads that loop's paired load as A colliding with B (both sat in bank 2 in that build), and
   H12 moved A into the empty bank 3 and saw no reliable change. A pair that reads two rows of
   one buffer collides in whatever bank the buffer sits, so that move could not have removed
   it -- a reason for H12's null that is independent of H11's (the core not being the critical
   path). It does not say which reason dominates.

6. The same data qualifies H11. i8i8_2x2 is this probe's re-typed copy of the 2x2 template
   H11 switched the int8 path to, and statically it is exactly H11's figures: an 8-bundle loop
   of 8 vmacs, 1.000 vmac/cycle, 32-byte frame. On one core it TIES the 4x2 in the k loop
   (20.008 against 20.000 cycles per unit K) because its 8-bundle body carries two same-buffer
   pairs to the 4x2's one, which eats the static gain exactly, and it is SLOWER per call at
   every K -- 2,717 / 3,998 / 6,559 against 2,447 / 3,735 / 6,295, a per-call intercept of
   1,436 against 1,167 cycles. Both move the same 128 C tiles per call; the 2x2 splits them over
   twice as many accumulator groups (32 against 16), each with its own k-loop prologue and
   epilogue, which is consistent with the higher intercept but was not isolated.
   docs/BENCHMARKS.md's H11 bullet says to keep the 2x2 lines as "free and strictly better"; on
   this core and these tiles they are neither, and a per-call loss of that size sits inside
   the +-2% the array showed. That is this probe's copy, one core, M = N = 64 -- not upstream's
   own 2x2 build and not the array. Separately, i8i8:unroll2 (the 4x2 with one raw pragma) is
   the best int8 schedule here: 18.0 cycles per unit K and 5,863 cycles per call at K=256, 6.9%
   under the control. It is a lever, not a recommendation: nothing here puts it on the array.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT ESTABLISH
----------------------------
- One core, M = N = 64, K = 64/128/256, one A/B/C placement. Not the array. The whole_array
  int8 GEMM's best measured tile, 64/128/64 at 2048^3 over 16 cores (4852.06 GOPS, 3,540.7 us
  NPU-bracket average; results/aie/gemm_tile_sweep_c_single_buffer_npu.log), spends about 6,224
  cycles per tile call per core (DERIVED: 3,540.7 us x 1.80 GHz / 1,024 calls per core). This
  probe's int8 control spends 3,735 on one call of that tile, so the kernel is ~60% of the
  array's per-call time, and the native kernel (2,791 at that tile) would cut at most ~944 of
  those cycles: <= 1.18x if everything else stayed fixed (DERIVED). The bandwidth half of W4A8 -- B
  at half the bytes through the shim and mem tile -- is the unmeasured part, and needs a
  whole_array derivative with packed B. That is the next step, not this one.
- The paired-load attribution (5) reads pointer roles off each loop's post-increments; it is not
  a resolved address trace. Exact where it resolves, unproven for the three native builds.
- int16 x int4 has no AIE2 mmul in aie_api and was not tried; bfp16 was not tried.
- Nothing here touches the accuracy of a W4 network: this is the arithmetic unit, not a
  quantization recipe.
- Trace cycles are clock-independent. Only the DERIVED array comparison uses 1.80 GHz.

METHOD
------
Static (compile only): kernels/w4a8_probe/static_probe.py compiles w4a8_kernels.cc and upstream
aie_kernels/aie2/mm.cc with the Peano command IRON's own compile step builds
(aie/utils/compile/utils.py, minus the -MD dependency-file pair) and reads each kernel's
hardware-loop body with tools/aie_disasm.py. --match-cache compiles upstream mm.cc over a dims
grid and compares against every IRON-built matmul_i8_i32 object left in ~/.npu/cache: 10 of 10
distinct cached bodies (41 objects) reproduced bundle for bundle. IRON defines neither
__chess__ nor __AIECC__, so every AIE_LOOP_* hint in aie_kernel_utils.h is empty in an IRON
build; the probe's k-loop modes are raw clang pragmas in the .cc (INNER_NO_UNROLL,
INNER_UNROLL2). mm_i8i8_local is upstream's 4x2 kernel source re-typed; Peano gives it a
different 9-bundle schedule from upstream's (rotation and register moves differ) with the same
8 vmacs, 8 loads and one A&A paired load. It is the control because upstream's own function
fires event0/event1 itself and cannot sit inside the wrapper's bracket.

Hardware: kernels/w4a8_probe/w4a8_probe.py -- one Worker on Tile(0, 2); A, B, C single-buffered
ObjectFifos; stack 4,096 B in every arm. The kernel wrapper zeroes C, brackets ONE kernel call
with event0/event1 (no DMA or lock inside the bracket), then emits 256 filler event pairs so
the packet reaches host memory; clock_probe's corrected frame decoder reads the stamps. A is
uniform int8 including -128 and 127, B uniform in [-8, 7] including both, for every kernel --
the int8 control too -- so one int64 reference serves all of them. After the first call the
kernel function inside IRON's built object is compared bundle for bundle against static_probe's
compile of the same source and defines: identical in all 54 processes. kernels/w4a8_probe/
sweep.py ran 9 arms x K in {64, 128, 256} x 2 repetitions, one fresh process per row (@iron.jit
keys its cache on the generator and its CompileTime args only, so a source hash goes in as
`rev`; two designs in one process once returned wrong data on this machine), arms rotated
within each K and K rotated across repetitions. Every (arm, K) cell returned ONE cycle count
across its 2 processes x 20 timed calls. kernels/w4a8_probe/fit.py fits cycles = a + b*K.

Gates: xrt-smi "No hardware contexts running on device" before the block, at the sweep's
start and at its end; tools/host_load.ps1 CLEAR (0.23 busy cores of 16, 17.8 GB free);
pmu_probe --calibrate reproduced 2.0003 and 9.0001 cycles/iteration against 2.000 and 9.000
(GATE 1 PASS); hwinfo_npu_bridge at 1 s for the whole block (witness_w4a8_probe.jsonl: 798
samples 11:06:00-11:19:17, never more than one hardware context, power mode Default in every
sample). The one other session on this machine confirmed it held no NPU context.

THE K LOOP, K = 128 -> 256 (computed from the per-K table in section E; exact two-point slope)
  arm                  cyc/unit K  MAC/cycle  vs control  static cyc/K  extra/K  extra/exec  same-buffer pairs (unresolved)
  i8i8:default           20.000      204.8      1.00x        18.0        2.0        1           1 (0)
  i8i8:unroll2           18.000      227.6      1.11x        16.0        2.0        2           2 (0)
  i8i8_2x2:default       20.008      204.7      1.00x        16.0        4.0        2           2 (0)
  unpack:default         17.008      240.8      1.18x        16.0        1.0        2           2 (0)
  unpack:no-unroll       18.008      227.5      1.11x        18.0        0.0        0           0 (0)
  unpack:unroll2         18.008      227.5      1.11x        17.0        1.0        1           1 (0)
  native:default         17.031      240.5      1.17x        16.0        1.0        1           0 (2)
  native:unroll2         11.000      372.4      1.82x        10.0        1.0        2           1 (3)
  native_2x2:unroll2     14.008      292.4      1.43x        13.0        1.0        1           0 (3)
MAC/cycle = 64*64 / slope. static cyc/K = (vmacs per unit K / vmacs per loop execution) x loop
bundles, from the IRON object's own loop (section E). extra/exec = the extra cycles per loop
execution. The pair counts are static_probe --paired on the K=128 builds (section D), which the
build check shows are the objects that ran.

